 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Short answer: no, not at the moment.** Inflation is above the RBA's 2–3% target and has been rising over the past year.\n\n**Latest inflation figures (August 2026, year-on-year)**\n- **Headline CPI:** 4.0%. It was 1.9% in June 2025 and peaked at 4.6% in March 2026. It dipped to 3.5% in July and then rose again.\n- **Trimmed mean:** 3.6%, up from 2.8% in June 2025. This is the RBA's preferred underlying measure. It has risen almost every month and has been above 3% since July 2025. That suggests the pressure is broad-based and not just a few volatile items.\n- **Rents:** 3.6%, down from 4.2% in mid-2025. Rent inflation is easing slowly.\n- **Electricity:** 13.2%. It was running at 20–37% for much of the past year, with a peak of 37.1% in October 2025. It is a major contributor to headline CPI.\n\n**How policy has responded**\n- The RBA cut the cash rate to about 3.6% in late 2025. From February 2026 it reversed course and raised it to about 4.35% by June 2026. The monthly average was 4.36% in September 2026.\n- The rate has been steady since June, which looks like a pause.\n\n**Labour market**\n- The unemployment rate has risen to 4.7% in September 2026, from about 4.1% in January. That is the highest in the period I checked.\n- The labour market is cooling, which should eventually ease price pressures. For now underlying inflation hasn't turned down.\n\n**Overall:** Underlying inflation of 3.6% is above the top of the target band and still drifting up. The rate rises and a softer jobs market haven't brought it back yet. Rents and the monthly headline readings are mixed, but the trend isn't yet one of inflation being under control. The next few monthly readings, especially trimmed mean, will show whether the tightening is working.",
